cli(["--help"])
```

I'd expect the help output to show `[INTEGER]` as the metavar in this case (and similarly TEXT/FLOAT/BOOLEAN/combinations for other choice value types), but it doesn't. Please fix the metavar generation for Choice options when show_choices=False.

task_prompts.click-c326df95:
When a command calls ctx.exit() (or exits eagerly from within an option callback), any callbacks registered via ctx.call_on_close() don't get run reliably, and in some cases callbacks can leak state or run in the wrong order relative to other eager option callbacks. For example:

```python
import click

@click.command()
@click.pass_context
def cli(ctx):
    ctx.obj = "test"

    @ctx.call_on_close
    def foo():
        print(click.get_current_context().obj)

    ctx.exit()
    click.echo("aha!")

cli.main([], standalone_mode=False)
```

I'd expect the call_on_close callback to still run (with the correct context still active) even though ctx.exit() was called before reaching the end of the command, and this should also hold when multiple eager options each register close callbacks and one of them triggers an early exit. Please fix context handling so close callbacks are always invoked properly on exit, in the correct order, without leaking state.

task_prompts.click-17874977:
When an option is defined with flag_value and envvar (with is_flag implicitly true), setting the environment variable to any value doesn't correctly set the option's value to the configured flag_value—instead it seems to use the raw env var content or otherwise mishandle it. For example:

```python
import click

@click.command()
@click.option("--upper", flag_value="upper", envvar="UPPER")
def cmd(upper):
    click.echo(upper)

cmd.main([], env={"UPPER": "whatever"}, standalone_mode=False)
```

Regardless of what the `UPPER` env var is set to (as long as it exists), I'd expect the output to be "upper" (the flag_value), but it isn't behaving that way. Please fix flag_value handling for options that use envvar.

task_prompts.click-884af5c2:
When using `click.option("--foo", is_flag=True, ...)` together with an explicit `type=bool` (or `type=click.BOOL`) and a `default`, the value passed to the command callback is wrong — passing `--foo` doesn't correctly toggle from the given default, and omitting it doesn't give the expected default either. For example:
```python
import click

@click.command()
@click.option("--foo", is_flag=True, type=bool, default=True)
def cmd(foo):
    click.echo(foo)
```
Invoking with `--foo` should print `False` and without it should print `True`, but the actual output is incorrect. Separately, defining a plain (non-flag) option whose `default` is an object that raises when converted to `bool` (e.g. a custom class whose `__bool__` raises `ValueError`) crashes the command entirely, even when the offending default is never used as a flag:
```python
class NonNegatable:
    def __bool__(self):
        raise ValueError("Cannot negate this object")

@click.command()
@click.option("--foo", default=NonNegatable())
def cmd(foo):
    pass
```
Running this command should succeed normally instead of raising an error. Please fix both issues.

task_prompts.click-b67832c2:
Defining a command parameter literally named `help` breaks argument parsing because it collides with click's automatic --help option. For example:
```python
import click

@click.command()
@click.argument("help")
def cli(help):
    click.echo(help)

cli(["value"])
```
This should print "value", but the parameter named `help` conflicts internally with the automatically generated help option, so parsing fails or behaves incorrectly. The same problem occurs with an option named `help` (e.g. `click.Option(['--assist'], 'help')`) or when a custom `help_option_names` is configured. Additionally, if a user parameter happens to use whatever internal name click reserves for its automatic help option, the collision should be reported with a clear warning instead of silently breaking. Separately, if a user explicitly defines their own `--help` option, it should replace the automatic one entirely rather than conflicting with it. Please fix the parsing so parameters named `help` (or reusing the help flag) work correctly.
